/**
 * \file
 * The core callback table's contents, as an X-macro list.
 *
 * Added 2026-09-25 (netsurf_upy, GPLv2 section 2(a) change notice).
 *
 * This is the whole of what a script-engine plugin may reach back into
 * the browser for.  It is included twice by `javascript/core.h` -- once
 * to declare `struct ns_script_core_v1`'s members and once, in the
 * browser only, to fill the table -- and it is the *only* place the
 * list is written.
 *
 * Three kinds, because they are spelled differently at the point of use:
 *
 *   NS_CORE_FN(n)    a function.  A plugin calls it as `n(...)`.
 *   NS_CORE_DATA(n)  a global object.  A plugin reads it as `n`.
 *   NS_CORE_RAW(n)   a global object that is only ever taken the address
 *                    of, in a *static* initialiser, so it cannot be
 *                    redirected by a macro at all -- the three nslog
 *                    categories, which `NSLOG()` puts in a `static
 *                    nslog_entry_context_t`.  `core.h` gives the plugin
 *                    its own `NSLOG()` instead.
 *
 * The list is not hand-written and must not be hand-extended.  It is
 * every symbol the duktape engine's objects leave undefined that the C
 * library does not define, measured with `nm --undefined-only` over the
 * 225 objects of the engine and its generated bindings.  The build
 * enforces it: the plugin is linked `-Wl,--no-undefined`, so a symbol
 * missing from this list is a link failure naming it, never a silent
 * lazy binding.
 */

/* --- functions ------------------------------------------------------ */
